# 🧮 List, Dictionary & Set Comprehensions

Comprehensions let you build a new collection from an existing one **in a single, readable line**.

---

## 🧮 List Comprehension

```python
squares = [n * n for n in range(5)]
print(squares)   # [0, 1, 4, 9, 16]
```

### Syntax

```python
[expression for item in iterable if condition]
```

```python
evens = [n for n in range(10) if n % 2 == 0]
print(evens)   # [0, 2, 4, 6, 8]
```

---

## 🧮 Dictionary Comprehension

```python
squares_dict = {n: n * n for n in range(5)}
print(squares_dict)   # {0: 0, 1: 1, 2: 4, 3: 9, 4: 16}
```

```python
names = ["Alice", "Bob", "Cara"]
name_lengths = {name: len(name) for name in names}
print(name_lengths)   # {'Alice': 5, 'Bob': 3, 'Cara': 4}
```

---

## 🧮 Set Comprehension

```python
unique_lengths = {len(word) for word in ["hi", "yes", "no", "ok"]}
print(unique_lengths)   # {2, 3}
```

Sets automatically remove duplicate results.

---

## 📊 Comprehension Comparison

| Type | Syntax | Result |
|-------|---------|---------|
| List | `[expr for x in iterable]` | Ordered, allows duplicates |
| Dict | `{key: value for x in iterable}` | Key-value pairs |
| Set | `{expr for x in iterable}` | Unordered, unique values |

